1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,562
This is a step by step guide to sharpening your own short track blades.

2
00:00:04,562 --> 00:00:13,753
You will learn how to clamp the blade, which stone does which job, how to remove the burr, how to polish for glide, and how to check your work.

3
00:00:13,753 --> 00:00:17,416
You can have the best boot and the best legs in the pack.

4
00:00:17,416 --> 00:00:22,686
But if your blades will not hold through the apex of a corner, none of it matters.

5
00:00:22,686 --> 00:00:26,670
Sharpening is the one job every short track skater has to own.

6
00:00:26,670 --> 00:00:28,920
Nobody can feel your edges for you.

7
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:32,881
A short track blade is not sharpened like a knife.

8
00:00:32,881 --> 00:00:35,163
You are not trying to make a point.

9
00:00:35,163 --> 00:00:41,947
You want two clean, square edges running the full length of the blade, with a flat surface between them.

10
00:00:41,947 --> 00:00:43,838
Those two edges bite the ice.

11
00:00:43,838 --> 00:00:47,490
As you lean, the blade rolls onto one edge or the other.

12
00:00:47,490 --> 00:00:53,621
How sharp that edge is decides how much sideways force you can apply before the steel lets go.

13
00:00:53,621 --> 00:00:58,252
When you sharpen, you take a small amount of steel off the flat bottom.

14
00:00:58,252 --> 00:01:01,187
You stop when the corners become crisp again.

15
00:01:01,187 --> 00:01:04,840
Everything else is about doing that evenly and squarely.

16
00:01:05,540 --> 00:01:07,794
Here are three edges, seen end on.

17
00:01:07,794 --> 00:01:09,386
On the left is the goal.

18
00:01:09,386 --> 00:01:11,840
A flat bottom with two crisp corners.

19
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:14,891
It holds under lean, and it reflects no light.

20
00:01:14,891 --> 00:01:16,814
In the middle is a dull edge.

21
00:01:16,814 --> 00:01:19,334
The corners have worn away to a curve.

22
00:01:19,334 --> 00:01:22,318
This is the blade that washes out mid corner.

23
00:01:22,318 --> 00:01:26,099
On the right is an edge that is sharp but carries a burr.

24
00:01:26,099 --> 00:01:29,746
That is a raised lip of steel left behind by the stone.

25
00:01:29,746 --> 00:01:33,924
It feels keen in the changing room, then folds over on the ice.

26
00:01:33,924 --> 00:01:42,016
The whole job is turning the middle and right shapes back into the left one, without removing more steel than you need to.

27
00:01:42,716 --> 00:01:45,564
Edges rarely go dull from the ice itself.

28
00:01:45,564 --> 00:01:46,398
Ice is soft.

29
00:01:46,398 --> 00:01:54,388
What rounds your corners is walking without guards, blade on blade contact, grit, and above all tiny spots of rust.

30
00:01:54,388 --> 00:02:00,364
In a humid climate, a wet blade left in a bag can lose its edge sitting in a cupboard.

31
00:02:00,364 --> 00:02:02,796
New skaters worry about stone grit.

32
00:02:02,796 --> 00:02:06,548
Experienced skaters worry about how the blade is held.

33
00:02:06,548 --> 00:02:12,593
If the blade is clamped even slightly twisted, you grind a hidden taper into the steel.

34
00:02:12,593 --> 00:02:17,734
You feel it as a blade that grips at the front and washes out at the back.

35
00:02:17,734 --> 00:02:24,613
So use a proper jig, tighten its knobs firmly every time, and set it on a table that does not rock.

36
00:02:25,313 --> 00:02:30,512
Once the blade is clamped straight, sharpening should be calm and repetitive.

37
00:02:30,512 --> 00:02:34,158
A combination stone has a coarse side and a fine side.

38
00:02:34,158 --> 00:02:38,480
Use the coarse side only when there is something real to remove.

39
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:42,464
A nick, a rust pit, or a corner you can see has gone round.

40
00:02:42,464 --> 00:02:45,300
Every pass takes steel you never get back.

41
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:48,203
Most sessions should live on the fine side.

42
00:02:48,203 --> 00:02:55,293
Lay the stone flat across the blade, keep it in full contact, and take long, even passes down the length.

43
00:02:55,293 --> 00:02:56,644
Watch your pressure.

44
00:02:56,644 --> 00:03:00,155
Most skaters press harder in the middle of a stroke.

45
00:03:00,155 --> 00:03:04,409
Over time that grinds a curve into a blade that should be flat.

46
00:03:04,409 --> 00:03:05,624
Let the stone cut.

47
00:03:05,624 --> 00:03:06,705
Do not force it.

48
00:03:07,405 --> 00:03:11,299
Sharpening pushes a thin lip of steel over the far corner.

49
00:03:11,299 --> 00:03:14,656
That is the burr, and removing it is not optional.

50
00:03:14,656 --> 00:03:18,886
Hold a diamond deburr stone flat against the side of the blade.

51
00:03:18,886 --> 00:03:23,922
Run it along the full length in a few light passes, then do the other side.

52
00:03:23,922 --> 00:03:26,674
You are knocking off a lip, not grinding.

53
00:03:26,674 --> 00:03:28,084
Next comes polishing.

54
00:03:28,084 --> 00:03:29,830
Sharpening gives you grip.

55
00:03:29,830 --> 00:03:33,657
Polishing gives you glide, and most club skaters skip it.

56
00:03:33,657 --> 00:03:37,148
With a polishing triangle, work the blue film first.

57
00:03:37,148 --> 00:03:41,378
When the friction drops, after about a minute, switch to green.

58
00:03:41,378 --> 00:03:42,855
Then finish with pink.

59
00:03:42,855 --> 00:03:45,877
The whole polish takes three to five minutes.

60
00:03:45,877 --> 00:03:48,160
A clean mirror means you are done.

61
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:50,778
Anything patchy, repeat the last stage.

62
00:03:51,478 --> 00:03:54,129
Here is the full order in six steps.

63
00:03:54,129 --> 00:03:54,424
One.

64
00:03:54,424 --> 00:03:59,063
Clamp both blades straight, then tighten the four knobs firmly.

65
00:03:59,063 --> 00:03:59,357
Two.

66
00:03:59,357 --> 00:04:04,291
Use the coarse side of the stone, but only if there is real damage.

67
00:04:04,291 --> 00:04:04,732
Three.

68
00:04:04,732 --> 00:04:10,918
Move to the fine side, with long, even passes and constant pressure from end to end.

69
00:04:10,918 --> 00:04:11,286
Four.

70
00:04:11,286 --> 00:04:15,999
Take the diamond deburr stone down both sides with light passes.

71
00:04:15,999 --> 00:04:16,367
Five.

72
00:04:16,367 --> 00:04:20,343
Polish with the triangle, blue, then green, then pink.

73
00:04:20,343 --> 00:04:20,638
Six.

74
00:04:20,638 --> 00:04:22,773
Check the edge under a light.

75
00:04:22,773 --> 00:04:25,645
Step two is the one to skip most weeks.

76
00:04:25,645 --> 00:04:30,210
Steps four and five are the ones skaters skip, and should not.

77
00:04:30,910 --> 00:04:34,353
Forget the thumbnail test people use on kitchen knives.

78
00:04:34,353 --> 00:04:36,106
There are two better checks.

79
00:04:36,106 --> 00:04:39,924
First, run a fingernail gently across the edge, not along it.

80
00:04:39,924 --> 00:04:44,744
Feel whether it catches the same way from the front of the blade to the back.

81
00:04:44,744 --> 00:04:49,251
Second, hold the blade under a light and look straight down at the edge.

82
00:04:49,251 --> 00:04:54,635
A dull edge shows a bright line, because a rounded corner has surface to reflect from.

83
00:04:54,635 --> 00:04:56,826
A properly sharp edge is invisible.

84
00:04:56,826 --> 00:05:01,082
Any bright spot along the length is a flat you have not reached yet.

85
00:05:01,082 --> 00:05:06,591
To check for a leftover burr, run a fingertip lightly along the flat side near the edge.

86
00:05:06,591 --> 00:05:08,657
It should feel completely smooth.

87
00:05:09,357 --> 00:05:11,573
Almost nobody is taught this part.

88
00:05:11,573 --> 00:05:12,942
Your stone wears too.

89
00:05:12,942 --> 00:05:18,222
It wears fastest in the middle, because that is where most skaters press hardest.

90
00:05:18,222 --> 00:05:20,047
On the left is a flat stone.

91
00:05:20,047 --> 00:05:25,783
It touches the full width of the blade and leaves a flat bottom with two square corners.

92
00:05:25,783 --> 00:05:29,563
On the right is a dished stone, worn hollow in the middle.

93
00:05:29,563 --> 00:05:35,495
Only the worn centre touches the steel, so it rocks across the blade and rolls the corners.

94
00:05:35,495 --> 00:05:41,362
You can spend a careful hour producing exactly the rounded edge you were trying to remove.

95
00:05:41,362 --> 00:05:44,555
The stone has to be flat before the blade can be.

96
00:05:45,255 --> 00:05:47,819
Checking for dishing takes ten seconds.

97
00:05:47,819 --> 00:05:51,172
Lay something straight along the face of the stone.

98
00:05:51,172 --> 00:05:55,313
A steel rule works, or the flat back of the polishing triangle.

99
00:05:55,313 --> 00:05:57,088
Hold the pair up to a lamp.

100
00:05:57,088 --> 00:06:00,834
If no light shows through, the face is flat, so carry on.

101
00:06:00,834 --> 00:06:05,041
If you see a sliver of light in the middle, the stone is dished.

102
00:06:05,041 --> 00:06:10,892
Flatten it before you touch a blade, or replace it if it is thin, cracked or deeply worn.

103
00:06:10,892 --> 00:06:13,784
Check along the length and across the width.

104
00:06:13,784 --> 00:06:18,451
Do this every second or third session, and always before a competition.

105
00:06:18,451 --> 00:06:26,799
Also wipe the stone before and after use, so steel dust does not clog it, and let it dry fully before it goes back in your bag.

106
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:31,165
There is no universal number for how often to sharpen.

107
00:06:31,165 --> 00:06:32,251
Go by the signs.

108
00:06:32,251 --> 00:06:43,995
Sharpen when the blade slides out at a lean it used to hold, when you skate a wider line through corners, when you see a bright line on the edge, or when you feel rust pits.

109
00:06:43,995 --> 00:06:47,118
Do not sharpen just because a week has passed.

110
00:06:47,118 --> 00:06:50,716
And never sharpen for the first time on race morning.

111
00:06:50,716 --> 00:06:53,703
Ride fresh edges for a session or two first.

112
00:06:53,703 --> 00:06:56,011
Rust costs skaters the most steel.

113
00:06:56,011 --> 00:07:01,102
Cold rink air meets warm, wet outside air, and blades collect condensation.

114
00:07:01,102 --> 00:07:04,225
Wipe them dry the moment you step off the ice.

115
00:07:04,225 --> 00:07:08,230
Let them reach room temperature before they go in a soaker.

116
00:07:08,230 --> 00:07:11,421
Never store them in wet soakers or hard guards.

117
00:07:12,121 --> 00:07:13,786
One last point on safety.

118
00:07:13,786 --> 00:07:21,512
A freshly sharpened blade is dangerous, and the risky moment is often when you unclamp it or reach across the bench.

119
00:07:21,512 --> 00:07:26,440
Wear cut resistant gloves for the whole process, including the tidying up.

120
00:07:26,440 --> 00:07:31,902
The skaters with the best edges are rarely the ones with the most expensive setup.

121
00:07:31,902 --> 00:07:41,026
They wipe their blades every time, check the edge under a light, keep the stone flat and clean, and sharpen when the blade tells them to.

122
00:07:41,026 --> 00:07:47,220
The full guide, with tool care tips and answers to common questions, is on the LiveLica blog.

123
00:07:47,220 --> 00:07:49,285
The link is in the description.
